A||||8||bzzzted||Brake conversion||||5||
Z||000000||bzzzted||06-18-2001||12:29 PM||||I am trying to gather information about brake conversions to get better braking on Fiero's, seems that main culprits of poor brakes seem to be 84-87, while 88 seems to have been given a better braking package. Maybe it is because I am somewhat mechanically defunct? but the whole GrandAm/ Beretta conversion seems off and somewhat complicated, what happens if i need new rotors? do i need to get them remachined after purchase to work again?<P>On the other hand why can i not convert rotors from an '88 to work on my 84-87 car? i undersand that there are changes and differences in the years but is it possible to make this work?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||06-18-2001||01:38 PM||||The GrandAm conversion uses unmodified GrandAm rotors and calipers mounted on the modified Fiero Hub.  If you need to replace the GrandAm rotors you simply buy new ones.  However you should read Ogre's notes about this conversion first and then judge for yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||06-18-2001||01:47 PM||||Since you use your Fiero rotors to make front hubs, they only need to be made once.  They should be an exact fit to the new GA rotors (which can be turned if needed later)so when buying a new set of rotors you will need to get ones that fit your hubs, bearing in mind there will be some variance in production tolerances, brands, etc.<P>Having said that you better read all the brake stuff at the Ogre's cave.  It's guaranteed to give us non-mechanical types a doozy of a headache.<P>Gary||||reg||4
Z||000003||1FST2M6||06-18-2001||01:54 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011101.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011101.html</A> <P>my findings on the Grand Am swap.. (SUPER easy.. and WORTH every red cent!!) ||||reg||1
Z||000004||PBJ||06-18-2001||01:56 PM||||no offence ment but I got a headache reading it too.  I get in and drive ours with GA brakes and what does the reading matter to all four tires stopping that fiero with no brake fade and little effort.   About the parking brake? sorry not avail with GA brakes but I doubt 20% of US have working p/brakes.  They bearly worked brand new!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Driven Visions||06-18-2001||09:13 PM||||do you use g-am rear rotors too?<P>What about a parking brake?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||06-18-2001||09:22 PM||||Depends. I am going to do a GA comversion on the front only, because I want to keep the parking brake. If you do the rear conversion, you loose the parking brake.||||reg||1
Z||000007||PBJ||06-19-2001||12:11 AM||||you use the GA frt rotors, pads, and calipers on the frt and the rear of the fiero and a master cylinder from a 94 full size pickup.  Do not do just the frt OR just the rear, and you must use truck master cylinder. It is not a little now a little later kind of swap.  If you want better brakes with out loosing the parking brake (that probably does not work anyways) buy QUALITY pads for all four corners.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/petesigsm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||06-19-2001||08:31 AM||||I did the GA conversion. Just did the rears a couple of weeks ago.<P>Front: when I had the hubs machined, I had the machinist press on a pilot ring which centers the beretta rotor properly. That is really the ONLY good way to do the fronts. I have him working on a second set which extends the pilot ring out and inch or so to insure that the wheel is properly centered. The beretta rotors are thick enough to cover most of the wheel pilot so that it no longer positively locates the wheel.<P>Rear: bolt, bleed, and go<P>With just the fronts, I could definitely tell that the bias was screwed up (I couldn't rotate the car with the brakes anymore   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) I wouldn't recommend doing just the fronts for a long period of time, unless you put a weaker bias spring in the proportioning valve. How much weaker? Experiment.<P>I haven't gone to the truck master cylinder yet. The pedal is actually higher than it was with the old barely functional parking brake. I haven't tried to purposely lock the wheels yet.<P>I'm still using stock rubber lines.<BR>I'm also using Performance Friction Carbomet pads.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
